scope: |
  Completing the owner's decision of 2026-08-10 -- `println(s.charAt(0))` prints the CHARACTER and
  a Char still counts as its code unit -- on the JS backend, which the decision names ("on all
  backends") and which today's work did not touch. The entry says a char LITERAL emits a plain JS
  string, so `'x' == 120` is false there while every other lane says true; the runtime already has
  a `_Char` box with `_isChar`, used for Char RESULTS but not literals.
  MEASURE FIRST on a fresh build: the shared checkout is stale, and its numbers are the old code.
next: full smoke on the rebased tree, then push both commits
done-so-far: |
  MEASURED first, on a fresh build: JS was wrong ONLY for a char in a NUMERIC context, and the
  boxing of charAt results was already right. So this is coercion, not a representation change --
  which matters, because JsGen carries an explicit note that char-as-string backs char/char
  equality, pattern matching and 46 name-keyed numeric-evidence sites, and that making the
  representation uniform needs its own corpus run. That note is correct and stands.
  Landed b37f7b0fc (literals) and one more commit pending (NAMES bound to a char literal -- the
  commoner shape, found by re-measuring rather than by reading). A declared param clears the new
  evidence: it is name-keyed and module-global, the same leak as js-char-int-eq-namescope-collision,
  and without the clear it emits charCodeAt on a NUMBER -- a TypeError.
  The negative control found two shapes I had missed by hand ("a"*2 -> aa, 1+"a" -> 1a).
  bugs-index-gate refused a duplicate slug on the way in: the type-test half is a SEPARATE entry
  (js-char-type-test-cannot-tell-Char-from-String) and stays OPEN -- it needs the box.
  Also repointed a sibling fixed-in orphaned by a rebase (50e1e2c7c -> 36777e7f6, same patch-id).
